WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.408
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.408 --> 00:00:10.341
The first memories I have as a kid
are the cozy Italian Sundays with

00:00:10.341 --> 00:00:12.506
my mom at the movie theater.

00:00:12.506 --> 00:00:16.823
My favorite movie of them
all was Disney Fantasia.

00:00:16.823 --> 00:00:20.024
I watched lazy Mickey Mouse
trying to teach that broom how to

00:00:20.024 --> 00:00:21.635
clean the sorcerer's cave.

00:00:21.635 --> 00:00:26.478
And I thought to myself how incredible
it would be if all things around me

00:00:26.478 --> 00:00:28.346
could simply come to life.

00:00:28.346 --> 00:00:32.917
If I could teach them to do all
the things I didn't want to do,

00:00:32.917 --> 00:00:36.307
including, guess what, tidying my room.

00:00:36.307 --> 00:00:40.314
Mickey Mouse's strategies seem
to be working out pretty well.

00:00:40.314 --> 00:00:44.063
The broom started cleaning the cave
autonomously, and so happy and

00:00:44.063 --> 00:00:46.375
relieved, Mickey simply went to sleep.

00:00:46.375 --> 00:00:49.170
There was really nothing to worry about.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:54.982
But a few hours later,
he wakes up to a completely flooded cave.

00:00:54.982 --> 00:00:58.211
The brooms had been repeating
the same action over and

00:00:58.211 --> 00:01:01.879
over again without realizing
what was going on around them.

00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:05.624
So Mickey begged them to stop,
but they just won't listen.

00:01:05.624 --> 00:01:08.034
And so
all he could do was tear them apart.

00:01:11.484 --> 00:01:14.484
Unfortunately, a few hours later,

00:01:14.484 --> 00:01:19.626
Mickey quickly realizes it's too late,
he has lost control.

00:01:19.626 --> 00:01:24.186
This time more and
more brooms start invading the cave again,

00:01:24.186 --> 00:01:27.526
desperately, trying to make it more clean.

00:01:27.526 --> 00:01:31.613
Fast forward, many years, I find
myself often going back to the story,

00:01:31.613 --> 00:01:35.849
as I try to think about the role of
artificial intelligence in our society.

00:01:35.849 --> 00:01:40.297
These intelligent algorithms promised
to bring speed, efficiency, and

00:01:40.297 --> 00:01:45.036
effectiveness, but ended up making
crucial decisions on our everyday lives.

00:01:45.036 --> 00:01:48.904
Who gets better education,
better health care,

00:01:48.904 --> 00:01:52.782
better financial support,
and who goes to jail.

00:01:52.782 --> 00:01:58.731
But just like we don't expect that broom
to understand what we mean by clean,

00:01:58.731 --> 00:02:00.744
it's up to us to decide and

00:02:00.744 --> 00:02:06.065
teach those algorithms what we mean
by right, by fair, and by good.

00:02:06.065 --> 00:02:09.894
The thousands of decisions of
this kind are made every day

00:02:09.894 --> 00:02:12.461
by data scientists around the world.

00:02:12.461 --> 00:02:15.594
What data to use,
what to optimize for, and

00:02:15.594 --> 00:02:19.247
when a machine learning
model is accurate enough.

00:02:19.247 --> 00:02:22.006
Well, I am a data scientist, and

00:02:22.006 --> 00:02:26.258
let me tell you one thing,
you should not trust me.

00:02:26.258 --> 00:02:28.806
And you should not leave it to
corporations, governments, or

00:02:28.806 --> 00:02:30.690
researchers to make these decisions for
you.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.259
And not because they intend harm, but

00:02:33.259 --> 00:02:37.921
because this is not a technology problem,
this is a values problem.

00:02:37.921 --> 00:02:43.879
And I believe all of us should have
a say in the role of AI in our society.

00:02:43.879 --> 00:02:45.631
And let me show you why.

00:02:45.631 --> 00:02:50.088
In February 2019,
Nijeer Parks was arrested.

00:02:50.088 --> 00:02:54.549
He was accused of shoplifting and
trying to hit a police officer.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:59.654
Turns out his face was identified
using a facial recognition software.

00:02:59.654 --> 00:03:06.112
But after ten days in jail,
the police found out that was a mistake.

00:03:06.112 --> 00:03:10.939
In August 2020, Shapiro is one
of thousands British students

00:03:10.939 --> 00:03:14.139
who saw their college admissions revoked.

00:03:14.139 --> 00:03:18.360
The UK government had just started
using a new AI tool instead

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.245
of final exams that was penalizing
struggling schools in poor areas as

00:03:23.245 --> 00:03:26.332
opposed to elite schools in wealthy areas.

00:03:26.332 --> 00:03:33.211
Today, Compass is one of the most widely
used AI tools in US criminal justice.

00:03:33.211 --> 00:03:37.668
So what Compass does is it helps judges
determine whether a defendant should be

00:03:37.668 --> 00:03:40.045
kept in jail or not while awaiting trial.

00:03:40.045 --> 00:03:45.103
And it does so by associating a score
from 1 to 10 to each defendant,

00:03:45.103 --> 00:03:48.381
based on more than 100 personal factors.

00:03:48.381 --> 00:03:52.666
If this score is high enough,
that defendant will be jailed.

00:03:52.666 --> 00:03:57.583
But analyzing the behavior of this
algorithm showed that bias data

00:03:57.583 --> 00:04:00.841
coming in produced bias results going out.

00:04:00.841 --> 00:04:03.824
Let's take Vernon and Brisha, for example.

00:04:03.824 --> 00:04:08.638
Brisha committed minor prior offenses and
no subsequent offense,

00:04:08.638 --> 00:04:13.120
yet as you can see her risk score
is much higher than Vernon's.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.680
So what does fair mean to us,
in this case?

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.963
Does it mean we want to apply
the same criteria to both of them?

00:04:20.963 --> 00:04:25.425
Or does it mean ensuring that
they're both equally likely to be

00:04:25.425 --> 00:04:27.873
subjected to algorithmic error?

00:04:27.873 --> 00:04:31.284
Because these two definitions
are equally valid but

00:04:31.284 --> 00:04:34.935
they have a very different
impact on society as a whole.

00:04:34.935 --> 00:04:38.594
So you see why deciding on rules and
values it's so hard,

00:04:38.594 --> 00:04:42.189
because they mean different things for
every one of us.

00:04:42.189 --> 00:04:43.622
Okay, I know what you're thinking.

00:04:43.622 --> 00:04:49.638
Isn't that why we elect our decision
makers to make these complex decisions for

00:04:49.638 --> 00:04:50.459
all of us?

00:04:50.459 --> 00:04:52.877
Well, in theory, that's true, but

00:04:52.877 --> 00:04:57.271
the problem with that is democracy
is not doing that well these days.

00:04:57.271 --> 00:04:59.701
In the past few years as a youth activist,

00:04:59.701 --> 00:05:03.037
I have worked hard with
the objective of involving as many

00:05:03.037 --> 00:05:07.429
young citizens as possible in
the democratic lives of their communities.

00:05:07.429 --> 00:05:11.651
And I could see how democracies
are continuing to weaken,

00:05:11.651 --> 00:05:16.577
institutions appear distant,
self-protective, unreliable.

00:05:16.577 --> 00:05:21.606
Only one in three Americans
trust their elected officials.

00:05:21.606 --> 00:05:27.307
The political class struggles to
understand the complexity of technology

00:05:27.307 --> 00:05:32.113
and to find consensus on the most
divisive matters of our time.

00:05:32.113 --> 00:05:36.548
We used to think that democracy happens
once every four years in a ballot, but

00:05:36.548 --> 00:05:38.402
it doesn't have to be this way.

00:05:38.402 --> 00:05:43.279
Researchers and activists have studied
a new model of engagement in the past

00:05:43.279 --> 00:05:45.956
few decades, deliberative democracy.

00:05:45.956 --> 00:05:50.533
And to see what that means,
let me take you back to Ireland 2015.

00:05:50.533 --> 00:05:55.591
80% of the Irish people approve
gay marriage in a referendum.

00:05:55.591 --> 00:05:58.282
But that journey started
two years earlier when

00:05:58.282 --> 00:06:01.580
the Irish parliament was facing
a controversial issue for

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:05.431
Ireland's history, and
decided it was time to try something new.

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:08.762
So they called a convention
on the constitution,

00:06:08.762 --> 00:06:13.773
composed of 66 ordinary citizens and
33 members of their parliament.

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:18.220
They came together, debated,
discussed, and some of those

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:23.617
recommendations were then turned into
referendums that became reality.

00:06:23.617 --> 00:06:25.634
So what happened here?

00:06:25.634 --> 00:06:30.268
While the world thought these
citizens were just too polarized,

00:06:30.268 --> 00:06:34.144
distant, and
frankly incompetent to have an impact,

00:06:34.144 --> 00:06:38.795
they actually transitioned from
observers to decision makers.

00:06:38.795 --> 00:06:43.714
And you can see and you can hear how
the narrative changed from they,

00:06:43.714 --> 00:06:46.828
the policy makers, to we, the citizens.

00:06:46.828 --> 00:06:52.623
Just like many activists around the world
that are doing already, we can influence

00:06:52.623 --> 00:06:58.108
the political agenda by becoming good
lobbyists for the causes we believe in.

00:06:58.108 --> 00:07:02.661
Deliberative democracy can happen
in many forms, citizens assemblies,

00:07:02.661 --> 00:07:05.199
town halls, participatory budgeting.

00:07:05.199 --> 00:07:10.874
And it has happened everywhere in the
world, from British Columbia to Karala,

00:07:10.874 --> 00:07:14.445
from Brazil to Denmark,
from Oregon to Belgium.

00:07:14.445 --> 00:07:18.332
In Italy, is trying to activate
the debate on climate change.

00:07:18.332 --> 00:07:19.408
And in California,

00:07:19.408 --> 00:07:23.536
citizen's assemblies were proposed to
solve the problem of homelessness.

00:07:26.014 --> 00:07:31.153
I guess what you're thinking,
this woman is trying to disrupt our

00:07:31.153 --> 00:07:36.317
institutions as we know them for
a new utopic, direct democracy.

00:07:36.317 --> 00:07:38.588
Couldn't be more wrong.

00:07:38.588 --> 00:07:43.384
I am suggesting we empower these
institutions every day with

00:07:43.384 --> 00:07:44.987
active citizenry.

00:07:44.987 --> 00:07:49.403
So how can deliberative democracy really
help us design the role of AI in our

00:07:49.403 --> 00:07:50.043
society?

00:07:50.043 --> 00:07:52.839
Well, first of all,
we'd have to define and

00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:57.414
select a sample of citizens that can
be representative of our diversity.

00:07:57.414 --> 00:08:01.241
And then we need to set up the process,
involve experts,

00:08:01.241 --> 00:08:07.012
secure our decision makers commitment, and
decide the questions we want to tackle.

00:08:07.012 --> 00:08:11.919
Because if we want to design a new bill
of digital rights, we'll have to agree

00:08:11.919 --> 00:08:16.612
on things like fairness, transparency,
openness, explainability.

00:08:16.612 --> 00:08:19.985
We'll have to decide how data
is transferred and owned.

00:08:19.985 --> 00:08:24.343
We'll have to decide who audits,
who controls who.

00:08:24.343 --> 00:08:29.486
It will not be an easy process, it will
require patience, experimentation, and

00:08:29.486 --> 00:08:34.718
trust in the process, even and most of
all, when we don't agree with the outcome.

00:08:34.718 --> 00:08:41.380
But ultimately, it'll help us understand
that beyond labels, we are simply humans.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:44.601
I am very, very passionate about AI.

00:08:44.601 --> 00:08:48.761
I really believe it can be an incredible
source of good for the world.

00:08:48.761 --> 00:08:51.205
Could AI be the next decision maker?

00:08:51.205 --> 00:08:56.497
Could AI find the best taxation
policy to optimize social welfare?

00:08:56.497 --> 00:08:59.993
Or could we just leave it to
AI to solve climate change?

00:08:59.993 --> 00:09:03.083
Maybe, we just don't know yet.

00:09:03.083 --> 00:09:07.219
But if and when AI will be
able to find those answers,

00:09:07.219 --> 00:09:10.420
we'll need to ask the right questions.

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:14.473
And when AI will be able
to find the best solutions,

00:09:14.473 --> 00:09:17.977
we will need to agree on
what we mean by best.

00:09:17.977 --> 00:09:22.799
All of us should have a say in
the role of AI in our society.

00:09:22.799 --> 00:09:26.085
Now I don't know how many of you
have seen Disney Fantasia, but

00:09:26.085 --> 00:09:27.892
here's a little spoiler for you.

00:09:27.892 --> 00:09:32.159
In the end, the sorcerer comes in and
saves the day.

00:09:32.159 --> 00:09:35.541
But the role of AI in our
society is no Mickey Mouse game.

00:09:35.541 --> 00:09:38.525
So please stay active, stay informed,

00:09:38.525 --> 00:09:42.844
participate in your democracy,
give it one more chance.

00:09:42.844 --> 00:09:47.515
Because this time there will be
no sorcerer coming to save us.

00:09:47.515 --> 00:09:52.205
This time we cannot afford to go to sleep.

00:09:52.205 --> 00:09:57.719
This time we can and
we must write the rules together.

00:09:57.719 --> 00:10:01.130
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:03.035
[MUSIC]

